---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Sportsbooks & Odds"
  description="Compare sportsbooks, understand odds formats, and learn how to find the best lines for your sports betting."
>
  <Hero
    title="Sportsbooks & Odds"
    subtitle="Understanding where and how to place your bets is fundamental to a successful betting strategy."
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <p>
          Choosing the right sportsbooks and understanding how odds work are foundational skills for any bettor. This guide covers the essentials of working with betting markets and maximizing the value of every wager you place.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Understanding Odds Formats" id="odds-formats">
        <h3>American Odds</h3>
        <p>
          American odds are expressed as positive or negative numbers. Negative odds (e.g., -150) indicate the favorite and show how much you need to bet to win $100. Positive odds (e.g., +130) indicate the underdog and show how much you'd win on a $100 bet.
        </p>

        <h3>Decimal Odds</h3>
        <p>
          Popular in Europe and increasingly common in North America, decimal odds represent your total return including your stake. Odds of 2.50 mean a $100 bet returns $250 ($150 profit plus your $100 stake).
        </p>

        <h3>Implied Probability</h3>
        <p>
          Every set of odds implies a probability. Understanding this conversion is essential for identifying value. For American odds, implied probability = Risk / (Risk + Potential Win) for favorites, and Potential Win / (Risk + Potential Win) for underdogs.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <Callout type="info" title="The Juice">
        <p>Sportsbooks build their profit margin into the odds, known as the "juice" or "vig." On a standard -110/-110 line, the true probability of each side is close to 50%, but the odds imply about 52.4% for each side—the difference is the book's edge.</p>
      </Callout>

      <ContentSection title="Line Shopping" id="line-shopping">
        <h3>Why It Matters</h3>
        <p>
          Different sportsbooks often offer different odds on the same event. Shopping for the best line can significantly impact your long-term results. Getting +115 instead of +105 on a bet might seem minor, but over hundreds of bets, these differences compound substantially.
        </p>

        <h3>Multiple Accounts</h3>
        <p>
          Maintaining accounts at multiple reputable sportsbooks gives you the ability to compare lines quickly and place bets where you're getting the best value. This is one of the simplest edges available to recreational bettors.
        </p>

        <h3>Timing Your Bets</h3>
        <p>
          Lines move throughout the week based on betting action and news. Understanding when to bet—whether getting in early on an expected line move or waiting for the market to settle—can be another source of value.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Choosing a Sportsbook" id="choosing">
        <h3>Key Factors</h3>
        <p>
          When evaluating sportsbooks, consider factors like competitive odds, market variety, deposit and withdrawal options, customer service, and the platform's usability. Legal, regulated books provide important consumer protections.
        </p>

        <h3>Bonuses and Promotions</h3>
        <p>
          Most sportsbooks offer sign-up bonuses and ongoing promotions. While these can provide value, always read the terms carefully. Wagering requirements and restrictions can significantly impact the actual value of these offers.
        </p>

        <h3>Bet Limits</h3>
        <p>
          Sportsbooks vary in how much they'll accept on different markets. Understanding these limits is important as your betting activity grows. Some books are known for accommodating larger bets, while others quickly limit winning players.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Market Types" id="market-types">
        <h3>Moneylines</h3>
        <p>
          The simplest bet type—pick the winner. Moneyline odds vary based on the perceived probability of each side winning.
        </p>

        <h3>Point Spreads</h3>
        <p>
          Spreads level the playing field by giving the underdog a points advantage. Betting the spread means your team must win by more than the spread (if favored) or lose by less than the spread (if underdog).
        </p>

        <h3>Totals (Over/Under)</h3>
        <p>
          Bet on whether the combined score of both teams will be over or under a set number. Totals betting requires understanding of pace, defensive strength, and situational factors.
        </p>

        <h3>Prop Bets</h3>
        <p>
          Proposition bets focus on specific events within a game, such as player statistics or specific occurrences. Props can offer value when your analysis differs from the market's assessment.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content {
    padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0;
  }
</style>
